course: chinese-street
lesson: 5
title: Names and "how old are you?"
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: On the first day at the park a girl asks you «nǐ jiào shénme míngzi?» (what's your name?). You say «wǒ jiào Sam» and throw it back: «nǐ ne?» (and you?).
explain: Chinese names put the family name first. Ming's full name is Wáng Míng: Wáng is the whole family's name, Míng is his own, and when his mum shouts «Wáng Míng!» across the flat, he is in trouble, just like in English.
explain: Grown-ups have their own rules: they ask each other «nín guì xìng?» (your honourable surname?), call younger friends Xiǎo Wáng (little Wang) and old mates Lǎo Lǐ (old Li), with warmth, not rudeness.
explain: Asking ages is different too. Kids ask «nǐ jǐ suì le?» but to a granny people say the very polite «nín duō dà niánjì le?», and in Chinese culture she may be proud to answer «bāshí le!» (eighty!).
fact: nǐ jiào shénme míngzi? («nee JYOW SHUN-muh MING-dz») means what's your name?; kids ask kids, while grown-ups ask you, not the other way round.
fact: wǒ jiào ... («waw JYOW») means my name is ...; jiào falls, while jiǎo (foot) and the jiǎo in jiǎozi (dumplings) dip.
fact: nín guì xìng? («neen GWAY SHING») asks politely for someone's surname; you answer only wǒ xìng ..., because guì (honourable) is only for the other person.
fact: wǒ xìng Wáng («waw SHING WAHNG?») means my surname is Wang; xìng sounds like the xìng in gāoxìng (happy), and context tells them apart.
fact: tā («tah», high) means he, she and it; spoken Mandarin has one sound for he and she, so Mandarin-speaking friends sometimes mix up he and she in English.
fact: nǐ jǐ suì le? («nee JEE SWAY luh») means how old are you?, used for children and young people; to grown-ups it sounds like talking to a toddler.
fact: wǒ jiǔ suì («waw JYO SWAY») means I'm nine; jiǔ (nine) dips, while jiù with a fall means just or then.
fact: nín duō dà niánjì le? is the very polite way to ask an old person's age; in Chinese culture it shows respectful interest.
fact: Xiǎo Wáng (little Wang) is a friendly name older people give to someone younger; a child never calls an adult Xiǎo anything.
fact: Lǎo Lǐ (old Li, said «láo lǐ» because of the two dips) is a warm name grown-ups use for a man their age or older; a child calls him Lǐ shūshu (Uncle Li).
fact: Many British Chinese children have a Yīngwén míngzi (English name) at school and a Chinese name at home; it is kind to ask which they prefer.
fact: In Wáng Míng the family name Wáng comes first and the given name Míng second.
exercise: How do you ask a kid's name? | nǐ jiào shénme míngzi? | nín guì xìng? | nǐ jǐ suì le? | nǐ qù nǎr?
exercise: Someone asks your name. You say | wǒ jiào ... | wǒ xìng guì ... | wǒ jiǔ suì | wǒ yǒu ...
exercise: After «wǒ jiào Sam», how do you ask back? | nǐ ne? | nǐ hǎo ma? | nǐ jiào ma? | nǐ shì?
exercise: Who says nín guì xìng? | Grown-ups asking each other's surname politely | Kids shouting to each other across the playground | Teachers telling off a class | Babies learning to talk
exercise: How do you answer nín guì xìng? | wǒ xìng Wáng | wǒ guì xìng Wáng | wǒ jiào guì Wáng | wǒ shì guì
exercise: What does tā mean? | He, she or it | You | I | Only they, all of them
exercise: Why might Ming say «she» about his dad in English? | Mandarin has one sound, tā, for he and she | He doesn't like his dad very much at all, really | It is polite in Chinese | It is a joke about dads
exercise: How do kids ask each other's age? | nǐ jǐ suì le? | nín duō dà niánjì le? | nǐ jiào shénme? | nín guì xìng?
exercise: «wǒ jiǔ suì» means | I'm nine | I'm ten years old | I just ate | I'm a ninja
exercise: Asking a granny her age in Chinese culture, said politely, is | Respectful interest | Always very rude indeed | Forbidden by law in China | Only for doctors and nurses
exercise: How do you ask a granny's age politely? | nín duō dà niánjì le? | nǐ jǐ suì le, nǎinai? | nǐ jiào shénme? | Lǎo Lǐ?
exercise: Ming's dad calls his young colleague Xiǎo Zhāng. What does it show? | Friendly warmth to someone younger | He thinks Zhang is very short and small | He is cross with Zhang | Zhang is a child
exercise: Can you, a child, call Ming's dad Lǎo Wáng? | No, you call him Wáng shūshu | Yes, it's the polite name for kids | Yes, if you shout it | No, you call him Xiǎo Wáng
exercise: What is Ming's family name in Wáng Míng? | Wáng | Míng, it comes last | Both, together | Neither, it is a nickname
exercise: Ming is called Kevin at school. What is a kind thing to do? | Ask which name he likes | Always use the Chinese one | Laugh at the English one | Make up a third name for him
exercise: Why is the polite surname question «honourable»? | guì honours the other person | Only rich people are ever asked it | It is a question for kings | guì means «what»
test: «ask his nan nǐ jǐ suì le, it's how you ask age» What is wrong? | That form is for kids; to her it's nín duō dà niánjì le? | Nothing, it is the perfect way to ask any granny her age | It means «what's your name» | You must never ask age in Chinese
test: You answer «nín guì xìng?» with «wǒ guì xìng Smith». Why do adults smile? | You called your own name honourable | You told them your age instead of your name | You said goodbye | You said «Smith» with a tone
test: «Wáng Míng, nǐ guò lái!» his mum shouts. What is likely? | He's in trouble — the full name! | She is introducing him to you and your mum | She is calling his dad | She forgot his name
test: Ming says «my dad, she's at work». What is going on? | Mandarin tā is he and she, so he slipped | Ming doesn't really know who his dad is | It's a rude joke | Ming means his mum
test: «his name's Míng Wáng innit, Míng first» Which is right? | No, the family name Wáng comes first: Wáng Míng | Yes, Chinese names always go given name first, like ours | Yes, Míng is the family name | No, it's Wang-Ming-Wang
test: What would happen if you called Ming's grandad «Xiǎo Wáng»? | It would sound very wrong; Xiǎo is for younger people | He would be delighted to feel so young again | It means «grandad» | It means «hello»
test: Ming says «wǒ jiù suì» with a falling jiù. What did you hear? | jiù is «just/then», not nine (jiǔ) | He is nine, it's the very same thing | He is ten | He said his name
test: At the park a lady calls a man «Lǎo Lǐ!». Is she being rude? | No, lǎo here is warm, like «mate» | Yes, she's calling him old and ugly | Yes, it's a swear word | No, she is his granddaughter
test: In a cartoon an English boy asks a teacher «nǐ jiào shénme míngzi?». What is off? | Too direct to a grown-up; adults ask you | Nothing, it's the most polite way to ask a teacher | He asked her age | He said goodbye
test: «wǒ jiǎo Sam» with a dip — what does Ming tease you about? | Dipping jiǎo is foot, not «am called» | Sam is not a Chinese name | You said your age | You said goodbye
test: Ming's granny proudly says «bāshí le!». What did she tell you? | She is eighty | She is only eight | She's very hungry | She's going out
exam: Identify the order in which the components of the name Wáng Míng are presented. | The family name precedes the given name | The given name precedes the family name | The order is determined by the season | The names are interchangeable without distinction
exam: Ascertain the appropriate reply of a person asked nín guì xìng. | wǒ xìng followed by the surname | wǒ guì xìng followed by the surname | wǒ jiǔ suì | nǐ jiào shénme míngzi
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of the enquiry nǐ jǐ suì le. | It is employed in enquiring the age of children and young persons | It is the most deferential enquiry addressed to elders | It is an enquiry regarding a person's surname | It is a valediction among adults
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appellation Lǎo Lǐ? | It is an affectionate form employed by adults for a man of comparable or greater age | It is an insult concerning the age of the addressee | It is the customary form by which children address adults | It designates a man younger than the speaker
